  "answer": "## MESH:D002945 — MESH:D009369\n2 papers, 2026.\n\n- 2026 · Journal of biochemical and molecular toxicology · PMID 42671218\n  \"Co-treatment with Luteolin significantly enhanced Cisplatin-induced growth inhibition in cancer cells while exerting limited cytotoxicity in normal cells.\"\n- 2026 · Scientific reports · PMID 42243346\n  \"Cisplatin (Cis) is a widely utilized chemotherapy drug for managing various cancers, but its efficacy is limited by nephrotoxicity, which frequently results in kidney fibrosis.\"\n  \"Fluvoxamine (FLV), a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor and sigma‑1 receptor agonist, lycopene (LYC), a carotenoid antioxidant, and their combination showed a novel protective influence on kidney fibrosis in male albino rats induced by administering Cis (7 mg/kg) by intraperitoneal (IP) injection once weekly for 4 weeks.\"\n\n## MESH:D002945 — MESH:D058186\n5 papers, 2026.\n\n- 2026 · Food science & nutrition · PMID 42662804\n  \"Cisplatin (CDDP) is a widely used chemotherapeutic agent, but its nephrotoxic side effects limit its clinical application, which can lead to severe acute kidney injury (AKI).\"\n- 2026 · Frontiers in microbiology · PMID 42293527\n  \"Cisplatin is a widely used chemotherapeutic drug for solid tumors, including colorectal cancer, but its clinical application is limited by dose-dependent nephrotoxicity, often resulting in acute kidney injury (AKI).\"\n  \"The gut-kidney axis has emerged as a key factor in cisplatin-induced AKI, with gut microbial imbalance contributing to inflammation and metabolic dysregulation.\"\n- 2026 · Frontiers in pharmacology · PMID 42232189\n  \"BACKGROUND: Cisplatin chemotherapy is limited by its nephrotoxicity, which leads to acute kidney injury (AKI).\"\n- 2026 · Frontiers in toxicology · PMID 42459863\n  \"Cisplatin-induced acute kidney injury remains a major dose-limiting complication in oncology.\"\n  \"This PRISMA-guided systematic review aimed to characterize the frequency, biological categorization, and co-reporting patterns of biomarkers used in human proximal tubule in vitro models of cisplatin-induced injury.\"\n- 2026 · International journal of molecular sciences · PMID 42353025\n  \"Associated with high morbidity and mortality, cisplatin-induced acute kidney injury (AKI) is a common clinical complication characterized by oxidative stress, inflammation, and mitochondria-associated signaling.\"",
